{
  "query": "You manage a high-traffic payment ledger for a regional retailer that stores its data in Cloud Spanner. A scheduled release includes a schema change and your business can tolerate less than 7 minutes of service interruption. How should you carry out the schema update to meet this requirement?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use Cloud Spanner backups to take a snapshot before the release, apply the schema change, and rely on restore if issues occur",
      "explanation": "This choice creates a safety net by taking a backup before making schema modifications and depends on restore for rollback",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Migrate the workload to Cloud SQL to modify the schema, then move the data back to Cloud Spanner",
      "explanation": "This choice moves data to a different managed database to perform the change and later brings it back",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Run the DDL directly on Cloud Spanner because it supports online schema changes without taking the database offline",
      "explanation": "This approach applies schema DDL on a live Spanner database and relies on its online schema update capability",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Set up Cloud Spanner change streams and a Dataflow pipeline to sync to another Spanner database, update the schema on the copy, then cut over the application",
      "explanation": "This approach builds a replication pipeline to another Spanner database for a cutover after the schema is modified on the copy",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Run the DDL directly on Cloud Spanner because it supports online schema changes without taking the database offline</b>.</p><p>This approach meets the requirement because <b>Cloud Spanner</b> performs most schema updates online. Operations such as adding columns or creating indexes run as long running background tasks while the database remains available to serve traffic. You can apply the change and monitor the progress without taking an outage, which comfortably fits a tolerance of less than seven minutes of disruption.</p><p><i>Use Cloud Spanner backups to take a snapshot before the release, apply the schema change, and rely on restore if issues occur</i> is not appropriate because restores create a new database and take significant time. A rollback through restore would far exceed the allowed interruption and would require complex cutover steps.</p><p><i>Migrate the workload to Cloud SQL to modify the schema, then move the data back to Cloud Spanner</i> is incorrect because moving between these different database platforms is complex and time consuming. It introduces far more risk and downtime than necessary and does not leverage the native online schema capabilities of Spanner.</p><p><i>Set up Cloud Spanner change streams and a Dataflow pipeline to sync to another Spanner database, update the schema on the copy, then cut over the application</i> is unnecessary and adds heavy operational complexity. This pattern is for change data capture and replication rather than online schema management, and the cutover could easily exceed the downtime budget.</p>",
  "batch_id": "119",
